Original commit body from 56df4cbc64c8821f120cbabf283994e9eece6c3b:

PR4 of the four-PR closure sequence for INV-fibis / INV-nuhun, and the second
half of the owner's 2026-08-23 bar: "name what it could not examine, AND AT
WHAT ANALYSIS FIDELITY".

Verdict carried eleven fields and NONE named the analyzer behind its edges,
while BoundaryCoverage's blind spots are every one a property of the EDGE SET.
Measured: two runs over one Rust crate differing ONLY in backend produced a
BYTE-IDENTICAL verdict block while one carried 10 origin=scip nodes and the
other zero. The raw material was always there and unread -- Edge.origin is a
list of pass IDs and Edge.__post_init__ HARD-RAISES on an empty one.

TWO DIFFERENT FACTS, KEPT APART:
  analysis_fidelity           WHAT RAN. Per verdict, language -> pass IDs,
                              scoped to CALL edges, because a `contains` edge
                              from the containment linker says nothing about
                              the fidelity of the call structure.
  higher_fidelity_available   WHAT COULD HAVE RUN. A statement about the
                              MACHINE, and the one that makes "rust-analyzer
                              installed but not enabled" distinguishable from
                              "no such backend exists".

NOT analysis_methods, which already exists and records how the taint walk
REASONED (ddg / ddg_mixed / structural). A structural verdict and a
tree-sitter verdict are different facts.

A FALSE SENTENCE IN THE FIRST CUT WAS CAUGHT BY MEASURING THE SECOND ARM.
Keyed on the call-edge fidelity map, the caveat fired in a run where
rust-analyzer WAS enabled and had printed its own "backend ACTIVE" banner --
because on that crate the SCIP pass emitted `references` and `contains` and no
external `calls`. It told the reader to enable something already on. "Did it
run" is now answered from a different population (passes_that_ran, every edge)
than "what produced the calls", with a test pinning the case.

ONE HOME FOR THE STAMP, because the count is the argument: this module builds
a ClaimVerdict at FIFTEEN return sites, and a field set at each is a field the
sixteenth will not set -- which is the omission WI-lagod exists to fix. Both
public entry points are thin wrappers that stamp once.

Availability is derived at the CLI and passed in: "is the binary on this
machine" is a fact about the machine, and putting a shutil.which inside a pure
function of the graph would make it depend on the host. Gated on the language
being present first, because probing RUNS the binary.

Measured end to end, and the arms now differ where they were identical:
  installed but OFF -> analysis_fidelity {"rust": ["rust"]} + the caveat
  ENABLED           -> same fidelity map, caveat GONE
The map is unchanged between them and that is correct -- on that crate SCIP
contributed no call edges, and the map reports what produced the call
structure, not what was switched on.

The mypy strict ratchet moved DOWN, not up: a duplicated bare `findings: list`
in the new wrapper tripped the shrink-only gate at 671; both occurrences (the
new one and the pre-existing one it copied) are now list[Any] and the baseline
is re-locked at 669.

